{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966784,"nonce":"gR3n2p5AcnMJNmx7gQSY//VsgGI=","title":"cn::switch had no deps() subscription — only its own click retargeted the thumb springs, so","message":"`cn::checkbox` wraps itself in\n`stateful::\u003cButtonState\u003e().deps([checked_state.signal_id()])`.\n`cn::switch` had NO `Stateful` and no `deps()` — it subscribed to\nnothing. Its thumb/track are live spring bindings, so it needs no\nrebuild to move, but something must call `set_target`. Only two paths\ndid: its own click handler, and `SwitchConfig::new` on construction.\n\nSo two controls bound to one signal disagreed: clicking the checkbox\nmoved nothing, and an FSM transition moved the switch only because a\nwhole-program `@stateful` wrap happened to rebuild everything. Scoping\nthat wrap to a [[project-dsl-with-blocks]] region removed the accidental\nsubscription and exposed it (2026-07-29).\n\nFix: one `reactive::effect` per bound signal, keyed like the springs\n(`RETARGETED` set on the raw signal id) so repeated construction does\nnot accumulate effects. It retargets both springs, so the thumb travels\nrather than snapping — no rebuild involved.\n\nWatch for: the effect MUST read via the passed graph, see\n[[gotcha-effect-closure-must-read-passed-graph]].\n\nTesting note: the signal registry is keyed by NAME and process-wide,\nand the switch's spring key derives from the signal id — so two tests\ndeclaring `signal busy` read each other's springs. Give every test its\nown signal name.","files":null}]}